.TH "UBERTOOTH\-TX" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-tx\fR \- retransmit symbols dumped with ubertooth\-dump
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-tx \-l LAP \-u UAP [\-t SECONDS] [\-U <index|serial>] [\-V]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Intended to retransmit symbols captured with ubertooth\-dump toward a target piconet identified by LAP/UAP\.
.P
\fBStatus: non\-functional\.\fR The tool currently performs no useful transmission (its own usage output warns "This tool currently does nothing")\. It is retained as a placeholder/experimental entry point\. Do not rely on it\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-l <LAP>\fR
target LAP (3 bytes / 6 hex digits); required\.
.TP
\fB\-u <UAP>\fR
target UAP (1 byte / 2 hex digits); required\.
.TP
\fB\-t <seconds>\fR
timeout; 0 means no timeout (default: 0)\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the host tools version, as \fBHost tools version: libubertooth <version> (<revision>), libbtbb <version> (<revision>)\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Transmitting uses the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level transmits at 7 (0 dBm), the CC2400's reset value\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-tx\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: LAP must be 6 hex digits (got 4466)\fR\.
.P
Both halves of the target address are required, and a command line missing either is refused there too, before any device is opened, so the message never depends on which boards are attached: \fBerror: \-l <LAP> and \-u <UAP> are both required\fR\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
What \fBubertooth\-tx\fR does happens over the air, so it writes no result: everything it prints, including the \fBSyncword: 0x\|\.\|\.\|\.\fR line, goes to standard error, and standard output stays empty\. Each error is one line starting \fBerror:\fR\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\. A bare \fBubertooth\-tx\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after the run is interrupted (Ctrl\-C) or its \fB\-t\fR timeout passes\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take and when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses the Bluetooth address the transmit needs, or takes only part of it: nothing is transmitted, since the address decides what the board sends\. libubertooth reports the refusal\.
